6511250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511314 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511314 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511314 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511314 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511321 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511400 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511426 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511504 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511504 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511584 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511584 #edb/ref 1074 "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511589 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522988 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522923 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/19689860cee70dfdb45a3d2f6708bcdab42b82b3ea20ea352ee0b2060fe7be13.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨7\\sin(t),\\,7\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0345c7e2-c321-58ac-aefe-5fccd5158803" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522923 #edb/ref 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨7\\cos(t),\\,-7\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}7\\cos(t)\\cdot 7\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}49\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522933 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522925 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e0/e0ac24d590f74a62bd45885b90190b82018372fd086d8cdda6ee8fde115d6fdd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨6{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,2{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04fa4661-86e7-5801-8b1a-25ba13abd1bf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522925 #edb/ref 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,-2\\sin(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{e}^{\\cos(t)}\\cdot 6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)+\\sin(t)}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523000 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522926 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3fc4346f3603d6d844584973c30b8ecf1e738a4d0a393ed1ab3ec62f764596cc.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨12\\sin(t),\\,12\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\t